The code statements that lead to the bug are:
xa_erase(&its->translation_cache, idx);
vgic_put_irq(kvm, irq);
Reason:
The code calls `xa_erase` but ignores its return value, instead using `irq` (the iterator variable) for `vgic_put_irq`. When multiple contexts concurrently invalidate the same cache, they may all see the same entry via iteration, then each calls `vgic_put_irq` on the same `irq`, dropping its single cache reference multiple times. This can cause the irq to be freed while still mapped by an ITE.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
irq = xa_erase(&its->translation_cache, idx);
if (irq)
    vgic_put_irq(kvm, irq);
Reason:
By assigning the result of `xa_erase` back to `irq`, only the context that actually removed the entry (where `irq` is non-NULL) will drop its reference. This ensures the cache’s single reference is released exactly once, preventing double-free even under concurrent access.

